From: onn field port (Walmart onn 4K Pro Gen 2, Mali-G310v2 MC1, kbase r44p1 CSF UK 1.20)
Subject: [PATCH csf-v10 014] pan/bi: finish compiling a very large shader

A fragment shader with 21141 SSA values, 2240 blocks and 27666 instructions
(register demand 137, the RT64 renderer's largest) did not finish compiling:
vkCreateGraphicsPipelines ran for more than five minutes at 100% CPU.

1. find_all_dominated_predecessors ran a fixed |blocks|-1 passes over every
   block for each loop header. Iterate to the fixed point instead, walking
   the blocks in reverse so successors are seen first. Same result set.

2. bi_register_allocate spilled in SSA form down to exactly the size of the
   register file. The allocator then still failed (vectors need contiguous
   registers) and its fallback spills one node per full pass: about 2.5 s a
   pass here, up to 2000 passes. A shader that has to spill at all now
   spills down to half the file. On this shader the targets 64, 56 and 48
   kept failing; 32 passed on the first full-file pass. Shaders whose
   demand fits the file are compiled exactly as before.

diff --git a/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bi_ra.c b/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bi_ra.c
index c5641e4384c..b1805991717 100644
--- a/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bi_ra.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bi_ra.c
@@ -1291,7 +1291,16 @@ bi_register_allocate(bi_context *ctx)
          if (ctx->inputs->is_blend)
             UNREACHABLE("Blend shaders may not spill");
 
-         bi_spill_ssa(ctx, regs_to_use);
+         /* Getting demand down to exactly the size of the file is not enough
+          * for the allocator below: vectors need contiguous registers, so
+          * it can still fail, and its fallback then spills a single node per
+          * full pass. On a shader with tens of thousands of values a pass
+          * takes seconds and the fallback allows 2000 of them. A shader that
+          * has to spill anyway spills down to half the file, which leaves
+          * that slack. Measured on a 21000-value shader with demand 137:
+          * targets 64, 56 and 48 kept failing, 32 passed on the first pass.
+          */
+         bi_spill_ssa(ctx, MIN2(regs_to_use, BI_MAX_REGS / 2));
          spill_count += bi_lower_spill(ctx, spill_count);
 
          /* By default, we use packed TLS addressing on Valhall.
diff --git a/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bifrost_compile.c b/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bifrost_compile.c
index 7322b404685..b5be50b68e3 100644
--- a/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bifrost_compile.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/compiler/bifrost/bifrost_compile.c
@@ -4652,15 +4652,26 @@ find_all_dominated_predecessors(const bi_context *ctx, bi_block *b,
 
    BITSET_CLEAR_COUNT(out, 0, ctx->num_blocks);
 
-   /* If the CFG was one long chain, we would require |blocks|-1 iters to
-    * propagate the in_loop info all the way through.
+   /* Propagate in_loop from successors to predecessors until nothing changes.
+    * The result is the same as running a fixed |blocks|-1 passes, which is
+    * what a CFG that is one long chain needs in the worst case, but a fixed
+    * count costs |blocks|^2 per loop header: minutes of CPU for a shader with
+    * thousands of blocks. Walking the blocks backwards visits successors
+    * first, so most loops settle in one pass plus one pass that finds no
+    * change.
     */
-   for (uint32_t iter = 0; iter < ctx->num_blocks - 1; ++iter) {
-      bi_foreach_block(ctx, block) {
+   bool progress = true;
+   while (progress) {
+      progress = false;
+      bi_foreach_block_rev(ctx, block) {
+         if (BITSET_TEST(out, block->index) ||
+             !BITSET_TEST(dominators, block->index))
+            continue;
+
          bi_foreach_successor(block, succ) {
-            if ((succ == b || BITSET_TEST(out, succ->index)) &&
-                BITSET_TEST(dominators, block->index)) {
+            if (succ == b || BITSET_TEST(out, succ->index)) {
                BITSET_SET(out, block->index);
+               progress = true;
                break;
             }
          }
